course: arts
lesson: 11
title: Concerts, orchestras and live gigs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Live music is people playing right in front of you, from an orchestra in a grand hall to a busker in the street to a rock band at a muddy festival, and every kind has its own habits.
explain: Picture walking into a concert hall: everyone is screeching and tooting at once; that is not the music but tuning up, like footballers stretching before a match; the oboe plays a note called A, everyone matches it, and the noise stops when the conductor walks on.
explain: Some habits are customs, not laws: many classical audiences wait to clap until the end of a long piece, rock crowds shout for an encore that was planned all along, and jazz players make up solos on the spot after years of practice.
explain: Music is tied to memory and feeling, so a song can make an old lady cry and laugh at once; and loud gigs need earplugs, while tickets should come from the official seller, not a tout.
explain: People talk about it in their own way: in Britain "grafting" is working hard, a "tout" (a "scalper" in America) resells tickets for big money, and "taking the mick" means taking advantage; in the North East "pet" means dear; Gen Z "goated" means the greatest of all time and "bussin'" means really good.
fact: Before an orchestra plays, the players tune up: usually the oboe plays the note A and everyone matches it so their instruments agree.
fact: Long classical pieces have several parts called movements, and many audiences wait until the end to clap; it is a custom, not a law, and in Mozart's time people often clapped between movements, and many musicians today are glad of the enthusiasm.
fact: Pop and rock concerts are loud and crowded; big festivals run for several days with many stages, and many fans wear earplugs.
fact: Musicians' earplugs make sound quieter but still clear, and many musicians wear them on stage.
fact: A busker is a street musician who plays for money people choose to give; many towns need a permit, and some famous musicians started out busking.
fact: The conductor keeps dozens of players together, deciding how fast, loud and gentle each part is, mostly in rehearsals, and shows it with the baton and face in the concert.
fact: Some people buy concert tickets only to sell them on for much more; many countries limit this, fake tickets are a real problem, and official sellers are safest.
fact: Opera is a play where the story is sung, with an orchestra; theatres often show translated words on a screen called surtitles, and many opera houses sell cheap tickets for young people.
fact: To improvise means to make up music as you play, within the song's chords and rhythm; good improvisers have practised for years.
fact: An encore is an extra song after the show seems finished, from the French for "again"; most big bands plan it in advance.
fact: Songs from someone's youth can bring back strong memories, which is why music is used in care homes and with people with dementia.
fact: Crying at music, from happiness or sadness, is normal.
exercise: Before the concert everyone screeches at once. What is happening? | The players are tuning up so their instruments agree | The orchestra is already playing the very first piece of the night | The players are rubbish and cannot play together | A fire alarm has gone off somewhere in the hall
exercise: Which instrument usually gives the note A for tuning? | The oboe | The big tuba | The bass drum | The triangle
exercise: Liam claps after the first part of a symphony. What is true? | Waiting till the end is a custom, not a law | He has broken a serious law of music and can be fined | Clapping is never allowed at any concerts | He must leave the hall at once, by the rules
exercise: What is a movement? | One section of a long piece of music | The way the conductor dances on the stage | The slow walk of the players onto the stage | A change of seats at half-time for the audience
exercise: Mei's sister came back from a festival with no voice. What is a sensible tip for festivals? | Protect your ears with earplugs and breaks | Stand right next to the big speakers all night | Earplugs ruin music, so never use them | Shout louder than the band all day so you can hear
exercise: Ravi's gran gives the busker a coin. What is busking? | Playing music in the street for money people choose to give | Begging in the street without doing anything in return | A crime in every town in the world, even with a permit | A kind of bus that plays music as it drives around town
exercise: Chloe says the orchestra can "just read the notes". Why do they need a conductor? | To keep everyone together and shape speed and loudness | The conductor is only there for show and the players ignore him | To turn the pages of the music for all the players | To sell the tickets at the door before the show begins
exercise: Tom's dad sees tickets online at three times the price. What is wise? | Buy from the official seller and be careful with resold ones | Any website's ticket is always real, so just buy the first one | Resold tickets are always the cheapest you can find | Touts are the safest place to buy, because they know the band
exercise: Anya cannot understand the Italian opera. What helps? | Surtitles, the translated words on a screen | Asking the singers to switch to English halfway | Leaving at the interval and going home | Covering her ears until the opera is over
exercise: Jamal sees a sax player make up a solo on the spot. What is it called? | Improvising, which takes years of practice | Random playing with no rules at all, which anyone can do | Reading the music very quickly | Miming along to a recording played on speakers
exercise: The band comes back after the crowd shouts "one more song". What is true? | The encore was usually planned in the setlist | The band only came back because the crowd begged | Bands never play again after they leave the stage | The crowd chose which song they would play next
exercise: Where does the word "encore" come from? | French, meaning "again" | Italian, meaning "very loud" | German, meaning "goodbye" | Spanish, meaning "dance"
exercise: An old lady cries, then laughs, at a care home concert. What is going on? | Songs from her youth brought back strong memories | Something is wrong, so the music must stop at once | She dislikes the children's singing and wants quiet | Music has no link to memory or feelings at all
exercise: Why is music used in care homes and with people with dementia? | It can bring back memories and feelings | It makes people forget everything that worries them | It is cheaper than paying for a good lunch | It is a rule set by law for all care homes
exercise: What is opera? | A play where the story is sung, with an orchestra | A rock concert in a field with lots of guitars | A song with no words at all | A dance done only to drums, with no singing at all
test: Your mate goes, "clap in the wrong place and they chuck you out, innit." Is he right? | No, it's a custom, and many musicians like the enthusiasm | Yes, it's the law in every concert hall and they'll call security | Yes, but only in Mozart's time | No, because clapping is banned entirely in every concert hall
test: Mei's sister says the festival was "bussin', no cap" (really good, honestly). What did she most likely need? | Earplugs and breaks to protect her ears | Nothing, festivals can't harm your ears at all | A louder speaker to hear the band better | A seat right by the stage, near the speakers
test: What would happen if an orchestra of eighty played without ever agreeing a tuning note? | Their notes would clash and sound out of tune | It would sound better than ever, all eighty of them | Nobody could hear the difference | The instruments would tune themselves after a minute
test: The uncle in the North East says, "Without her we'd be a train crash, pet." What does he mean? | Without the conductor the orchestra would fall apart, dear | The conductor causes accidents when she waves her stick about | The orchestra travels to concerts by train | His pet dog plays in the orchestra on Sundays
test: Ravi's gran says the busker is "grafting for every penny" (working hard). What does she think busking is? | Real work that deserves a coin or a clap | Begging that the police should stop at once | A lazy way to get money | A crime against the town and its shops
test: Tom's dad growls that the touts are "taking the mick". What is he cross about? | People reselling tickets for much more money | The band playing far too loud for his ears | The tickets being given away free | The concert starting an hour early without warning
test: Jamal says the sax player is "goated" (the greatest). His mate says it's "just random notes". Who is closer? | Jamal, because good improvising follows the song's rules | The mate, because improvising has no rules at all | Neither, because jazz is always written down note by note | The mate, because sax can't play solos
test: Ella's cousin rolls his eyes: "That encore was planned." What is true? | Most big bands plan the encore, but cheering still matters | The band only returns if the crowd cries real tears | Encores are always a surprise to the band, never planned | Bands are not allowed to plan anything before a show
test: Anya's grandad points at the screen above the stage. Why? | It shows the words translated from Italian | It shows the football score for the bored | It tells people exactly when to clap and cheer | It shows the price of the tickets
test: Someone says opera is "only for posh old people". What is a fair answer? | Many theatres sell cheap tickets for young people | He's right, children are banned from every opera | Opera is only ever sung in English | Opera tunes never appear in cartoons, films or adverts
test: A cartoon shows the orchestra warming up and the audience cheering the "amazing music". What's funny about that? | The warm-up noise is only tuning, not the music | Warm-ups are the best part of a concert | Orchestras never warm up | The audience should have booed the conductor
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the sounding of the note A by the oboe prior to an orchestral performance. | The bringing of all instruments into agreement of pitch | The commencement of the first movement of the work | The signalling of the interval to the members of the audience | The demonstration of the oboist's proficiency
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning applause between movements of a symphony. | Its avoidance is a custom rather than a law, and it was common in Mozart's time | It is prohibited by law in every jurisdiction | It has never been practised by any audience | It results invariably in expulsion from the hall
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "improvisation" in jazz? | The invention of music in performance within the chords and rhythm of the piece | The random production of notes without regard to any rule or to the piece | The rapid reading of music from a written score | The miming of a performance to a recording previously made in a studio
exam: Ascertain the course of action most prudent in respect of the purchase of tickets for a popular concert. | The purchase from the official vendor, with caution towards resold tickets | The purchase from any website whatsoever without enquiry | The purchase solely from persons reselling at a profit | The abandonment of any attempt to attend
